&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(108)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(143)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 13pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Total other (deductions) income, net&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;4,105&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;6,840&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:91.517%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:60.526%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.574%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:17.600%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr style="height:3pt"&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:91.517%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:60.526%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.574%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:17.600%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr style="height:3pt"&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="display:none"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Note 14. Related Party Transactions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Certain Networks subsidiaries, including NYSEG, borrow from AGR, the parent of Networks, through intercompany revolving credit agreements. For NYSEG, the intercompany revolving credit agreements provide access to supplemental liquidity. See Note&#160;6 for further detail on the credit facility with AGR. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Avangrid Service Company provides administrative and management services to Networks operating utilities, including NYSEG, pursuant to service agreements. The cost of those services is allocated in accordance with methodologies set forth in the service agreements. The cost allocation methodologies vary depending on the type of service provided. Management believes such allocations are reasonable. The charge for operating and capital services provided to NYSEG by AGR and its affiliates in the three months ended March&#160;31, 2026 and 2025 was approximately $112.8 million for 2026 and $36.1 million for 2025. The charge for services provided by NYSEG to AGR and its subsidiaries in the three months ended March&#160;31, 2026 and 2025 were approximately $22.2 million for 2026 and $6.9 million for 2025. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;All charges for services are at cost. All of the charges associated with services provided are recorded as revenues to offset other operating expenses on the financial statements. The balance in accounts payable to affiliates of $31.7 million at March&#160;31, 2026 and $20.1 million at December&#160;31, 2025 is mostly payable to Avangrid Service Company. The balance in accounts receivable from affiliates of $2.8 million&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;at March&#160;31, 2026 and December&#160;31, 2025, is receivable from various companies. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;he was no balance in notes receivable from affiliates at March&#160;31, 2026. The balance in notes receivable from affiliates of $0.1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;million&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;at December&#160;31, 2025 is&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; due from CMP. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Networks holds an approximate 20% ownership interest in the regulated New York TransCo. Through New York TransCo, Networks has formed a partnership with Central Hudson Gas and Electric Corporation, Consolidated Edison, Inc., National Grid, plc and Orange and Rockland Utilities, Inc. to develop a portfolio of interconnected transmission lines and substations to fulfill the objectives of the New York energy highway initiative, which is a proposal to install up to 3,200 MW of new electric generation and transmission capacity in order to deliver more power generated from upstate New York power plants to downstate New York.&#160;In 2016 NYSEG received approximately $67 million from New York TransCo in the form of $43 million for assets constructed and transferred to the New York TransCo, $22 million in contributions in aid of construction and approximately $2 million in advanced lease payments for a 99 year lease of land and attachment rights. As of March&#160;31, 2026 and December&#160;31, 2025, we had no outstanding receivable balance from New York TransCo.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In February 2025, NYSEG&#8217;s certain eligible storm restoration costs were financed through a storm cost securitization completed pursuant to a financing order issued by the New York Public Service Commission. The securitization was executed through a bankruptcy remote, special purpose subsidiary, NYSEG Storm Funding, LLC (NYSEGSF), wholly-owned by NYSEG. NYSEGSF was established for the sole purpose of holding storm recovery property and issuing storm recovery bonds. In February 2025, NYSEGSF issued $711 million of storm cost recovery bonds at interest rates ranging from 4.71% to 5.16% and final maturity ranging from May 1, 2031 to May 1, 2037, and used the proceeds to acquire storm recovery property from NYSEG.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;NYSEG acts as servicer and billing agent for NYSEGSF and continues to bill customers the approved storm recovery charges through a separate line item on customer bills. NYSEG is entitled to collect a servicing and an administrative fee for routine billing, collection and reporting activities as an administrator and servicer for NYSEGSF pursuant to an administration and a servicing agreement between NYSEG and NYSEGSF. Amounts collected are remitted to the collection accounts of NYSEGSF through short-term notes payable with affiliates accounts. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The charges for servicing and administrative services provided by NYSEG to NYSEGSF were approximately $0.2 million for the three months ended March&#160;31, 2026 and $0.6 million for 2025, and are recorded as other revenues in the financial statements. The balance in notes payable to affiliates was $12.9 million and $13.3 million at March&#160;31, 2026 and December&#160;31, 2025, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
